  <!-- ═══ TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY ═══ -->
  <div class="translator-box">
    <div class="translator-label">✦ Translator's Summary</div>

    <p>This halacha (a single unbroken passage of seventeen clauses) reveals the inner dimension of the <em>Chalah</em> separation through the Torah <em>"Tefilah l'Chavakuk"</em> (Likutay I:19). The attribution <em>"as is brought in the words of our master, may his light shine"</em> (<em>kamuvah b'divrei Rabbeinu nero ya'ir</em>) appears five times, always pointing back to Rebbe Nachman's own teachings.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Why water triggers the obligation (Clauses 1–2):</strong> Flour = drawn from <em>gevuros</em> (the north-table, rains of severity). Water = waters of <em>chasadim</em>. Their union in the dough creates <em>da'as</em>. Bread is the food of <em>da'as</em>. The moment <em>da'as</em> is present the <em>klipa</em> of the Tree of Knowledge grips hardest — even though <em>terumos</em> and <em>ma'asros</em> already partially rectified the grain, a new and deeper rectification is now required against the <em>se'or</em> (leaven) that clings to the dough specifically.</li>
      <li><strong>Chalah = Reishis = Leshon HaKodesh (Clauses 3–5):</strong> <em>Chalah</em> is called <em>reishis</em> — just as the holy tongue is <em>reishis</em> (it was the instrument of creation). The holy tongue is when speech (<em>dibur</em>) is connected to <em>da'as</em> and drawn from <em>chochmah</em>. The Tree of Knowledge sin was exactly the opposite: "one thing in the mouth, another in the heart" — speech severed from wisdom. Giving the <em>Chalah</em> (= the letters clothed in the dough) to the Kohen (= <em>chochmah</em>) re-welds speech to its root. The wholeness of the holy tongue and the rectification of the covenant (<em>tikkun habris</em>) are mutually dependent.</li>
      <li><strong>Chavah and women (Clause 6):</strong> Chavah's sin blemished the holy tongue. Women repair this by separating <em>Chalah</em> — and by praying at that moment. The word <em>chalah</em> itself means prayer (<em>vayechal Moshe</em>).</li>
      <li><strong>Adam HaRishon and prayer (Clauses 7–8):</strong> Prayer = <em>Malchus</em> = the divine speech-faculty = <em>chayei sha'ah</em> (the vitality of this world). Adam HaRishon was the <em>Chalah</em> of the world: through his prayer, rain fell and creation was completed. The world was created through the holy tongue.</li>
      <li><strong>The Omer measure (Clause 8 cont.):</strong> <em>Ephah</em> = intellect = the beginning of thought, still above speech. <em>Omer</em> = 1⁄10 of <em>ephah</em> = the tenth and final level in the chain descending from <em>chochmah</em> to spoken speech. When the dough reaches <em>omer</em>-size, the full descent from wisdom to word is present — obligating <em>Chalah</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Clauses 9–10</strong> are two short, distinct closing statements confirming what was established.</li>
      <li><strong>Outside the Land (Clauses 11–14):</strong> Eretz Yisrael = holy tongue. The lands of the nations = languages of the nations = speech without <em>da'as</em> = blemish of the Tree of Knowledge. There the <em>Chalah</em> is immediately defiled and cannot be connected to the Kohen-<em>chochmah</em>. It must be burned — subdued by fire, not elevated.</li>
      <li><strong>Five species / closing (Clauses 15–17):</strong> Five corresponds to the <em>heh</em> of <em>da'as</em> (as brought in <em>Rabbeinu nero ya'ir</em>). The dough-union of water (<em>chasadim</em>) and flour (<em>gevuros</em>) = <em>da'as</em>. Then <em>Chalah</em> = the <em>reishis</em> of that <em>da'as</em>.</li>
    </ul>
